# Python generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest-tmp/
.scan_tmp/

# Build artifacts. `build/` holds a full copy of src/, so leaving it unignored
# would commit a duplicated source tree that silently drifts from the real one.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# Local virtual environments and experiment outputs
.venv/
runs/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation: opt-in, not opt-out.
#
# Only the user-facing documents ship: README.md and docs/guide.md. Everything
# else under docs/ and examples/ is working material — it carries this machine's
# absolute paths, measured-once numbers, failure narratives and internal
# planning, none of which belongs in a public repository.
#
# Ignoring the whole directory and then re-including the one public file is
# deliberate: a *new* internal doc is then private by default. An allowlist
# cannot leak something that was never published on purpose, whereas a denylist
# silently publishes every file someone forgets to add.
#
# requirements/ is NOT ignored: it holds the pinned dependency set that the
# README's install command needs (`pip install -r requirements/...`).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/*
!docs/guide.md
# examples/ is entirely local, including RUNBOOK.md (a reproducibility checklist
# for this machine, not user documentation).
examples/

# Durable conclusions live in docs/guide.md; the working notes stay local.
docs/engineering-notes.md

# AGENTS.md is instructions to the repository's AI coding agents and maintainer:
# personal working habits, machine-specific paths (venv names, model cache with a
# pinned revision, local run directories) and links to the other local docs
# above. Public readers would get dead links and someone's private setup notes.
# Contributor-facing conventions, if ever published, belong in CONTRIBUTING.md.
AGENTS.md

# Installation and execution logs
*.log
.scripts/
